Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period adventure drama, naturalistic faces, fur, frost, and fabric; Klondike-era 1897–1899.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, poplars, a wide cool veranda. BUCK, a massive hundred-forty-pound dog—St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd mix, thick warm coat, proud head—bounds through grass with the Judge's grandsons.

(SOUND: children laughing, distant fountain splash, birds in the poplars)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing. Men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. They wanted dogs.

Buck plunges into the big cement swimming tank, shakes water in a glittering spray, then stalks past fox terriers like a king.

(SOUND: water slap, terriers yelping)

EXT. ORCHARD PATH - NIGHT

Moon through bare branches. MANUEL, a gardener's helper in rough clothes, walks with Buck on what seems a stroll. Buck trusts him.

(SOUND: gravel under boots, night insects)

They reach a little flag station. A STRANGER waits. Money chinks between hands.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls. The rope tightens. He springs; the stranger grapples his throat and throws him. Buck's tongue lolls; eyes glaze.

(SOUND: rope cinch, Buck's choked snarl, train whistle far off)

EXT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

A cagelike crate. Buck, dazed, brass collar filed off, flung inside. The SALOON-KEEPER peers in by tallow candle.

(SOUND: file on brass, crate slam, waterfront foghorn)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He could not understand what it all meant. He felt oppressed by a vague sense of impending calamity.

EXT. SEATTLE HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - DAY

Four men set the crate down. THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER—stout, sweater sagging at the neck—signs a book, takes hatchet and club.

(SOUND: crate wood splintering, men scrambling onto the wall)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck launches one hundred forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air. Again and again he charges; again the club smashes him down. Blood from nose and mouth. A final shrewd blow—Buck crumples senseless.

(SOUND: club impacts, Buck's scream-snarl, wall-top shouts)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

He pats the pounded head. Buck endures it. Water. Raw meat from the man's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver. That club was his introduction to primitive law.

Other crated dogs arrive. Strangers pay money and lead dogs away. A little weazened man, PERRAULT, eyes Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. Seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Cold gray sea. Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, stand with Perrault and FRANCOIS—a swarthy French-Canadian half-breed. White mush falls from the sky. Buck sniffs, licks—it bites like fire and is gone.

(SOUND: propeller throb fading, wind, men's laughter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

Chaos of tents, huskies, clubs. Curly makes friendly advances to a wolfish husky. A leap, a metallic clip of teeth—her face ripped open. Thirty huskies close in a silent circle. She goes down. The mass buries her, screaming.

(SOUND: teeth clip, sudden snarling mass, Curly's agony, axe and clubs scattering dogs)

Francois swings an axe. Too late. Curly lies limp in bloody snow. SPITZ, a big snow-white dog, runs out his scarlet tongue as if laughing. Buck's eyes fix on him with deathless hate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you.

Francois fastens a harness on Buck. Dave the wheeler nips his hindquarters. Spitz leads. Buck learns "ho," "mush," the swing of the trail.

(SOUND: harness buckles, whip crack, sled runners on snow)

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

EXT. CAMP ON THE TRAIL - NIGHT

White plain. Buck driven from the tent with curses and cooking utensils. He digs where Billee curls under snow—warm air, a lesson. He digs his own hole and sleeps.

(SOUND: wind nip, snow crunch, distant dog quarrels)

At dawn he bounds up through a flashing cloud of snow, buried alive in the night's fall.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Bleak camp under rock wall. Spitz steals Buck's nest. Buck springs—fury. They circle, snarling.

(SOUND: warning snarl, sudden pandemonium of starving huskies)

Starving village huskies—skeletons in draggled hides—pour in for the grub. Clubs rise. Buck fights three at once. Spitz attacks him treacherously from the side. The team flees onto the ice.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The dominant primordial beast was strong in Buck. Under the fierce conditions of trail life it grew and grew.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK BED - NIGHT

Wan moonlight. A snowshoe rabbit flees. Buck leads sixty dogs in full cry—blood lust, the old wolf-cry. Spitz cuts across, breaks the rabbit's back. Buck drives in upon him. Circle of huskies waits, breaths drifting upward.

(SOUND: pack cry, rabbit shriek, then ghostly calm—only panting and snow under feet)

Fang clashes fang. Spitz is practised; Buck streams blood. Then imagination—Buck sweeps low, crunches the left fore leg. Bone breaks. Then the right. Spitz staggers on three legs. The circle tightens.

Buck springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. Spitz disappears under the dark circle of huskies.

(SOUND: bone crunch, final rush, brief thrashing, then silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Francois finds Spitz missing, Buck covered with wounds.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? Dat Buck two devils. An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

Buck claims the lead spot. Francois tries Sol-leks; Buck drives him back. Club raised—Buck circles just out of range, wise in the way of clubs. At last Francois throws the club down. Buck swings into the lead.

(SOUND: traces fastening, sled break-out, runners singing)

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY (MONTAGE)

Buck at the head. Record run—lakes, rapids, White Pass. Then the mail train: heavy loads, endless miles. DAVE, sick, cries out in the traces, bites through to reclaim his place, pulls till he can no more. A revolver shot behind river timber. Buck knows.

(SOUND: sled bells, whip snaps, one gunshot, then bells again)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Eighteen hundred miles will tell upon the toughest. They were dead tired—the slow strength drainage of months of toil.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Worn dogs sold. HAL, nineteen, belt bristling with cartridges; CHARLES, watery-eyed, fierce mustache; MERCEDES, soft and pretty, Charles's wife. Overladen sled, chaos, whip.

(SOUND: whip whistle, dogs' strained panting, Mercedes's sobs)

NEIGHBOR MAN
They're weak as water. Plum tuckered out. They need a rest.

HAL
Rest be blanked.

The top-heavy sled capsizes on the turn. Later: Outside dogs added, food short, Dub shot, Billee axed in the traces. Buck's coat draggled, ribs sharp under loose hide. He staggers at the head.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - MOUTH OF WHITE RIVER - DAY

Spring sun. Dogs drop as if struck dead. JOHN THORNTON, weathered, kind-eyed, whittles an axe-handle. Hal whips the downed team. Buck will not rise. Club falls. Thornton springs—animal cry—hurls Hal backward.

(SOUND: whip, club on flesh, Thornton's inarticulate cry, Mercedes scream)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton knocks the knife away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. Hal, Charles, Mercedes, and the remaining dogs pull out onto the ice. A section gives way—scream—yawning hole. Gone.

(SOUND: ice crack and roar, then river hush)

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck heals. SKEET, a little Irish setter, washes his wounds. NIG, huge black hound, good-natured. Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, shakes him, murmurs loving oaths. Buck's eyes laugh.

(SOUND: river run, birds, Skeet's soft whine)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. John Thornton had saved his life. He was the ideal master.

Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his mouth—fierce, a caress. He will not let Thornton from his sight.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON STREET - DAWSON - DAY

Sixty below. Crowd banked around a sled loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour—runners frozen fast. MATTHEWSON's wager. Thornton kneels, cheek to Buck's cheek.

(SOUND: crowd murmur dying to silence, frost creak)

JOHN THORNTON
(whispering)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee!

Buck swings right—crackling under runners.

THORNTON
Haw!

Left—snapping, sled pivoting free.

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. Muscles knot under silk fur. Claws scar the snow. Inch by inch—then steadily—a hundred yards. Roar of the crowd. Thornton on his knees, shaking Buck, cursing him softly and lovingly.

(SOUND: sled lurch, crowd explosion, hats in air)

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
I'll give you a thousand for him, sir—twelve hundred!

JOHN THORNTON
No, sir. You can go to hell, sir.

EXT. LAKE COUNTRY GOLD CAMP - DAY

Months later. Moose-hide sacks of gold piled like firewood beside the pan. Buck muses by the fire. A vision of a short-legged hairy man. From the forest, a call.

(SOUND: pan gravel, fire crackle, then a long-drawn howl unlike any husky)

EXT. FOREST TRAIL - TWILIGHT

Buck meets a long lean timber wolf—wild brother. They sniff noses, run side by side through twilight. Buck remembers Thornton and turns back. The wolf howls mournfully behind him.

(SOUND: paws on soft earth, then a mournful lone howl)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The call filled him with a great unrest. But the love for John Thornton drew him back to the fire again.

EXT. SPRUCE-BOUGH LODGE VALLEY - DAY

Buck returns from a moose kill. Fresh trail. NIG dead with an arrow. A sled-dog thrashing. HANS feathered with arrows. YEEHAT warriors dance about the wrecked lodge.

(SOUND: sing-song chant, then Buck's fearful roaring)

Buck becomes a hurricane. Throat of the chief—jugular fountain. Another. Arrows fly; Indians shoot one another in panic. They flee, crying of the Evil Spirit.

Pete dead in his blankets. Skeet at the pool's edge. Buck follows Thornton's trace into the muddy water—no trace leads away.

(SOUND: pursuit crashing away, then wind over empty camp)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. It left a great void in him which food could not fill. The last tie was broken.

EXT. MOONLIT CLEARING - NIGHT

Full moon. A wolf pack pours in like a silvery flood. Buck stands motionless. The boldest leaps—Buck breaks its neck. Three more try; throats and shoulders slash open. The pack crowds, confused. Buck at bay against a gravel bank.

(SOUND: yelps, snap and gash, then lull—panting tongues)

A long lean gray wolf advances—the wild brother. They touch noses. An old battle-scarred wolf sits, points his nose at the moon, and howls. The pack howls. Buck sits and howls with them.

(MUSIC: the long wolf howl rising into a chorus—the song of the pack)

They spring into the woods. Buck runs at the head beside his wild brother, yelping as he runs.

EXT. THE VALLEY - SUMMER DAY / WINTER NIGHT

Summer: a great gloriously coated wolf stands alone where yellow stream once sank from rotted moose-hide sacks. He howls once, long and mournfully, and departs.

Winter night: under pale moonlight or glimmering borealis, he runs at the head of the pack, leaping gigantic, great throat a-bellow.

(SOUND: mournful solo howl; then full pack song under the borealis)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
